// LKIF Core 1.1: the graph model and sample data
//
// Generated by ../../cypher.py from model.json and sample.json. Edit those and regenerate, rather than this file.
//
// A fictional residential tenancy, read through LKIF Core's legal and commonsense layers. A member of a legislative
// body introduces a bill, and the body enacts a statute bearing three norms: an obligation on landlords to keep a
// dwelling in repair, a permission for tenants to withhold rent once they have given notice of disrepair, and a
// prohibition on evicting without a court order; the behaviours the norms qualify are compared as obliged, allowed
// and disallowed. A landlord and a tenant play their roles in the context of a lease, which bears the tenant's
// obligation to pay rent and which the tenant promised; the tenant's written notice, borne by a letter, leads to the
// landlord's repairs. Each action has its actor, requirement, result and the moment it took up, and the moments are
// ordered in time.
//
// The data leaves the minimum counts of TemporalOccurrence_IMMEDIATELY_AFTER_TemporalOccurrence open: LKIF time is
// serial, so every moment has one immediately before and one immediately after it; the sample covers a finite stretch
// of time, so its first and last moments have no neighbour on one side.
//
// Run it against an empty database of Neo4j 5 or later, Enterprise Edition, for its node key and property existence
// constraints, e.g. with cypher-shell -f model.cypher. It merges every node and relationship, so it can be run again.

// The model: its constraints

CREATE CONSTRAINT uri_Thing_key IF NOT EXISTS FOR (n:Thing) REQUIRE n.uri IS NODE KEY;
